{
  "id": 21,
  "name": "regret_bounded",
  "intent": "This heuristic applies a saturating transform to regret, bounding its maximum contribution near 0.4. Unlike linear regret weighting, which can be dominated by extreme values from sparse regions or outliers, the bounded form treats all high-regret candidates similarly while preserving urgency ranking among typical candidates. The hypothesis: very large regret values often indicate poorly-conditioned instances where linear weighting causes myopic detours. Distance remains the dominant signal; regret provides bounded look-ahead to avoid painting into corners, yielding more uniform behavior across diverse instance geometries while staying within the regret family.",
  "tags": [
    "regret",
    "nonlinear"
  ],
  "code": "def priority(f):\n    d = f['distance']\n    r = f['regret']\n    den = 1.0 + r\n    if den < 1e-9:\n        den = 1e-9\n    regret_term = 0.4 * r / den\n    return -d + regret_term",
